course: dictionary
lesson: 5001
title: Industrial: a word with a history
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to a subject, like history or geography, and they carry that subject's meaning. «Industrial» is one of these words. It means to do with factories, making goods, and industry. Look at the words around it to find its meaning, just as you would with any new word.
explain: «The Industrial Revolution began in Britain around 1760, when people began making things with machines in factories instead of by hand at home.» The clues are factories and machines. So the Industrial Revolution was the time when factories and machines changed how things were made.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. «In industrial England, soot darkened trees.» If you swap in "factory England", it still makes sense, and soot from factory chimneys fits. Industrial can also describe strong, large things made for factory use, like «industrial machines».
fact: Industrial means to do with factories, making goods, and industry.
fact: An industrial place has lots of factories.
fact: The Industrial Revolution began in Britain in the late 1700s, and coal powered it.
fact: Industrial also describes things that are strong and large enough for factory use.
fact: Geographers study industrial areas, industrial estates and industrial pollution.
exercise: «The Industrial Revolution began in Britain around 1760.» What does industrial mean here? |*To do with factories and machines making goods |To do with sea and boats |To do with farming crops and animals |To do with the sun and weather
exercise: «In industrial England, soot darkened trees.» Where did the soot most likely come from? |Snowflakes |Fallen leaves |*Factory chimneys |Rain clouds
exercise: Which of these is an industrial place? |A quiet village with one shop |*An industrial estate full of factories |A forest with tall pine trees |A beach with rock pools
test: «The Arsenal of Venice was a huge state shipyard that could build ships one after another, almost like a factory.» What does «almost like a factory» tell you? |*It made ships quickly, one after another, in a factory-like way |It was a place where people went to sleep |It sold fish at a market |It was built out of ice
test: Which phrase best describes «industrial machines»? |Small toys for children |*Strong, large machines made for factory use |Old wooden carts pulled by horses |Machines that play music
exam: Jayden says «industrial» is only a history word. What is the best answer? |No, it is only a sports word for games played in a stadium |*It is used in history and geography, and it means to do with factories and making goods |It only means old and dusty |It means happy and loud
